First, let's determine the total area covered by the fog bank and then figure out how long it will take to cover half of that area.

The entire city spans 40 miles across from the ocean front to the opposite inland edge. Therefore, if each mile covers 2 miles horizontally within the city (to account for both sides), then:
\[ \text{Total horizontal length} = 40 \text{ miles} \times 2 = 80 \text{ miles} \]

Given that it takes 15 minutes to cover 2 miles horizontally:

\[
\text{Time per mile horizontally} = \frac{15 \text{ minutes}}{2 \text{ miles}}
=7.5 \text{ minutes per mile}
\]

Now calculate the total time required to cover all 80 miles:

\[ \text{Total coverage time} = 80 \text{ miles} \times 7.5 \text{ minutes per mile} 
 = 600 \text{ minutes}
\]

Next, find the time needed to cover half of this distance:

\[
\text{Half coverage distance} = \frac{80}{2}\text{ miles }=\ 40 \text{ miles}
\]
Using the same rate:

\[
\text{Time required to cover half the city} = 40 \text{ miles} \times 7.5 \text{ minutes per mile}
=300 \text{ minutes}
\]

So the final calculation gives us that it will take **300 minutes** to cover half of the city.
Answer: 300(minutes)
